<sn><md>### Important Terminology

| Term            | Meaning                                                             | Example                |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- |
| **Rclone**      | The command-line program used to manage and transfer files          | `rclone`               |
| **Remote**      | A configured connection to a storage service                        | `gdrive:`              |
| **Backend**     | The code/interface Rclone uses to communicate with a storage system | Google Drive, S3, SFTP |
| **Provider**    | The actual storage service                                          | Google Drive           |
| **Local**       | Files stored directly on your computer                              | `/home/emon/Documents` |
| **Path**        | Location of a file or directory                                     | `gdrive:Documents`     |
| **Remote path** | A path inside a configured remote                                   | `gdrive:Backup`        |
| **Source**      | Where files are copied/synced from                                  | `/home/emon/Documents` |
| **Destination** | Where files are copied/synced to                                    | `gdrive:Documents`     |
| **Object**      | A file managed by an object-storage backend                         | `photo.jpg`            |
| **Directory**   | A folder containing files/objects                                   | `Documents/`           |
| **Config file** | Stores Rclone remote configurations and credentials                 | `rclone.conf`          |
| **Command**     | An operation performed by Rclone                                    | `copy`, `sync`, `move` |
| **Filter**      | Rules controlling which files are included/excluded                 | `--exclude "*.tmp"`    |
| **Checksum**    | A value used to compare file contents                               | MD5, SHA-1             |
| **Transfer**    | Moving/copying data between source and destination                  | PC → Drive             |
| **Mount**       | Makes a remote appear as a filesystem/folder                        | `rclone mount`         |
| **Bisync**      | Two-way synchronization feature                                     | `rclone bisync`        |
| **Crypt**       | Rclone's encrypted remote backend                                   | `crypt:`               |
| **VFS**         | Virtual File System layer used by commands such as `mount`          | `rclone mount`         |
| **Cache**       | Temporary/local data used to improve certain operations             | VFS cache              |
| **Config**      | Configuration containing remote definitions and settings            | `rclone config`        |
</md></sn><sn><md>### General Concept

The most important concept to understand is a **remote**.

For example, you can configure Google Drive with the name `gdrive`:

```text
gdrive:
```

Then a folder inside it can be addressed as:

```text
gdrive:Documents
```

A file can be addressed as:

```text
gdrive:Documents/notes.pdf
```

So:

```text
gdrive:
```

means the **root of the remote**, while:

```text
gdrive:Documents
```

means the **Documents directory inside that remote**.

### **Source and Destination**

Most Rclone commands follow this general structure:

```bash
rclone <command> <source> <destination>
```

For example:

```bash
rclone copy ~/Documents gdrive:Documents
```

Here:

| Component          | Meaning     |
| ------------------ | ----------- |
| `rclone`           | Program     |
| `copy`             | Command     |
| `~/Documents`      | Source      |
| `gdrive:Documents` | Destination |

The same architecture allows you to do:

```text
Local → Google Drive
Google Drive → Local
Google Drive → OneDrive
S3 → Google Drive
SFTP → Google Drive
Local → S3
```

This is one of Rclone's major advantages: **the commands remain mostly the same even when the underlying storage provider changes.**
